%I A402115 #9 Nov 22 2026 17:03:47
%S A402115 0,1,2,8,12,27,40,74,97,156,204,296,374,508,637,828,1005,1271,1513,
%T A402115 1888,2211,2672,3109,3718,4266,5000,5705,6634,7478,8619,9655,11003,
%U A402115 12276,13851,15380,17261,19006,21194,23316,25851,28229,31167,33921,37280,40504,44242
%N A402115 a(n) is the number of tangential quadrilaterals, up to congruence, with integer side lengths, integer area, and semiperimeter n.
%C A402115 Congruence includes reflections. By Pitot's theorem, opposite sides sum to n, so every side-length class has a representative (i, j, n - i, n - j), where 1 <= i <= j <= floor(n/2).
%C A402115 The inradius equals the area divided by n and is therefore rational, but need not be an integer. The quadrilaterals counted by A402114 form a proper subclass for every n >= 2.
%C A402115 For tangent lengths (t, i - t, j - i + t, n - j - t) and area k, one has k^2 = n*(2*i*(n - j)*t + i*(n - j)*(j - i) - n*t^2). This quadratic gives at most two admissible solutions for fixed side lengths and area.
%C A402115 Every integer area from 1 to floor(n^2/4) is attained by a tangential quadrilateral with integer side lengths and semiperimeter n. Indeed, the side lengths (v, v, w, w), where v = floor(n/2) and w = ceil(n/2), suffice. Hence a(n) >= floor(n^2/4) for n >= 2.
%C A402115 For the formula, put h = floor(n/2), p = i*j*(n - i)*(n - j), l = n*i*(n - j)*(j - i), and u = n*i*j*(n - i - j). Here [condition] equals 1 if the condition holds and 0 otherwise.
%C A402115 The asymptotic formula follows by Riemann summation, since integer-rounding corrections contribute only lower-order terms. The leading constant is the same as in A402114.
%H A402115 Felix Huber, <a href="/A402115/b402115.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..400</a>
%H A402115 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/TangentialQuadrilateral.html">Tangential Quadrilateral</a>.
%F A402115 a(n) = Sum_{1 <= i <= j <= h, 2*j = n} (floor(sqrt(p)) - floor(sqrt(l))) + Sum_{1 <= i <= j <= h, 2*j < n} (2*floor(sqrt(p)) - floor(sqrt(l)) - floor(sqrt(u)) - [p is a square]).
%F A402115 a(n) ~ c*n^4, where c = Integral_{y = 0..1/2} Integral_{x = 0..y} (2*sqrt(x*y*(1 - x)*(1 - y)) - sqrt(x*(1 - y)*(y - x)) - sqrt(x*y*(1 - x - y))) dx dy = 0.00863321215757...
%e A402115 a(2) = 1: The unit square has area 1, semiperimeter 2 and inradius 1/2.
%e A402115 a(4) = 8: The side-length classes (1, 1, 3, 3), (1, 2, 3, 2) and (2, 2, 2, 2) contribute 3, 1 and 4 quadrilaterals, respectively.
%p A402115 A402115 := proc(n)
%p A402115     local a, i, j, h, p, l, u, q, s, t;
%p A402115     h := iquo(n, 2);
%p A402115     a := 0;
%p A402115     for i from 1 to h do
%p A402115         for j from i to h do
%p A402115             p := i*j*(n - i)*(n - j);
%p A402115             l := n*i*(n - j)*(j - i);
%p A402115             q := isqrt(p);
%p A402115             s := isqrt(l);
%p A402115             if q*q > p then q := q - 1 end if;
%p A402115             if s*s > l then s := s - 1 end if;
%p A402115             if 2*j = n then
%p A402115                 a := a + q - s;
%p A402115             else
%p A402115                 u := n*i*j*(n - i - j);
%p A402115                 t := isqrt(u);
%p A402115                 if t^2 > u then t := t - 1 end if;
%p A402115                 a := a + 2*q - s - t;
%p A402115                 if q^2 = p then a := a - 1 end if;
%p A402115             end if;
%p A402115         end do;
%p A402115     end do;
%p A402115     a;
%p A402115 end proc:
%p A402115 seq(A402115(n), n = 1 .. 46);
%Y A402115 Cf. A273771, A402114.
%K A402115 nonn,new
%O A402115 1,3
%A A402115 _Felix Huber_, Nov 13 2026
